#v(0.8em)

I will show you the truth of this in the following particulars.

#strong[#emph[The first device that Satan has to draw souls from holy duties, and to keep them away from religious services, is,]]

By presenting the world to the soul in such a dress and in such a garb as to ensnare the soul and to win over the affections of the soul.#footnote[The beauty of the world overthrows a Christian more than its strength; the flattering sunshine more than the blustering storm. In storms we keep our garments close about us.] He represents the world to them in its beauty and splendor, which proves a bewitching sight to a world of men. It is true that this did not take hold of Christ, because Satan could find nothing in him for his temptation to work on. But Satan finds something in us for this temptation to work on, so that he no sooner casts out his golden bait than we are ready to play with it and to nibble at it; he no sooner throws out his golden ball than men are apt to run after it, though they lose God and their souls in the pursuit. Ah! how many professing Christians in these days have for a time followed hard after God, Christ, and#footnote[The people who live by the Nile are made deaf by the noise of the waters; so the world makes such a noise in men’s ears that they cannot hear the things of heaven. The world is like the swallows’ dung that put out Tobias’s eyes.] ordinances, until the devil set before them the world in all its beauty and splendor. This has so bewitched their souls that they have come to have low thoughts of holy things, and then to be cold in their affections toward holy things, and then to slight them, and at last, like the young man in the gospel, to turn their backs on them. Ah, the time, the thoughts, the spirits, the hearts, the souls, the duties, the services that the inordinate love of this wicked world eats up and destroys, and has eaten up and destroyed! Where one thousand are destroyed#footnote[The champions could not wring an apple out of Milo’s hand by strength, but a fair maid got it at once by fair means.] by the world’s frowns, ten thousand are destroyed by the world’s smiles. The world, like a Siren, sings to us and sinks us; it kisses us and betrays us, like Judas; it kisses us and strikes us under the fifth rib, like Joab. The honors, splendor, and all the glory of this world are only sweet poisons, which will greatly endanger us, if they do not eternally destroy us. Ah, the multitude of souls who have gorged themselves on these sweet baits and died forever!

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[The prior whom Melanchthon tells of rolled his hands up and down in a basin full of gold coins, thinking by this to charm away his gout, but it would not work.] of Satan is to dwell on the impotence and weakness of all these things below. They are not able to secure you#super[+] from the least evil. They are not able to obtain for you#super[+] the least desirable good. The crown of gold cannot cure the headache, nor the velvet slipper ease the gout, nor the jewel about the neck take away the toothache. The frogs of Egypt entered the houses of the rich men of Egypt as well as of the poor. Our daily experience shows#footnote[Nugas the Scythian, despising the rich presents and ornaments that were sent to him by the emperor of Constantinople, asked whether those things could drive away calamities, diseases, or death.] this: all the honors and riches, etc., that men enjoy cannot free them from the colic, the fever, or lesser diseases. Indeed, what may seem strangest of all is that a great deal of wealth cannot keep men from falling into extreme poverty. In Judg 1:6, 7 you#super[+] will find seventy kings with their fingers and toes cut off, glad, like puppies, to lick up crumbs under another king’s table; and shortly after, the same king who brought them to this penury is reduced to the same poverty and misery. Why then should that be a bar to keep you out of heaven which cannot give you the least ease on earth?

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell on the vanity of all worldly good, as well as on its impotence. This is the sum of Solomon’s sermon: “Vanity of vanities! Everything is vanity.” This#footnote[Gelimer, king of the Vandals, led in triumph by Belisarius, cried out, “Vanity of vanities! Everything is vanity.”] our first parents found, and therefore they named their second son Abel, that is, #emph[vanity.] Solomon, who had tried these things and could best tell their vanity, preaches this sermon over again and again: “Vanity of vanities! Everything is vanity.” It is sad to think how many thousands there are who can say with the Preacher, “Vanity of vanities! Everything is vanity,”#footnote[The fancy of Lucian, who places Charon on the top of a high hill, viewing all the affairs of living men and looking on their greatest cities as little birds’ nests, is very pleasant.] indeed, can swear it, and yet follow after these things as if there were no other glory or happiness but what is to be found in the very things they call vanity. Such men will sell Christ, heaven, and their souls for a trifle; they call these things vanity, but do not heartily believe them to be vanity, and set their hearts on them as if they were their crown, the height of all their royalty and glory. Oh, let your#super[+] souls dwell on the vanity of all things here below, until your#super[+] hearts are so thoroughly convinced and persuaded of their vanity as to trample on them, and make them a footstool for Christ to mount up and ride in holy triumph in your#super[+] hearts.#footnote[Oh, the imperfection, the ingratitude, the fickleness, the inconstancy, the treachery of those created things we most slavishly love!] Chrysostom once said that if he were the fittest man in the world to preach a sermon to the whole world gathered together in one congregation, and had some high mountain for his pulpit, from which he might have all the world in view, and were furnished with a voice of brass, a voice as loud as the trumpet of the archangel, so that all the world might hear him, he would choose to preach on no other text than that in the Psalms: “O mortal men, how long will you#super[+] love vanity and seek after lies?”#footnote[Ah! if we would only weigh man’s pains against his payment, his afflictions against his blessings, his miseries against his pleasures, we would then see that there is nothing gained by the bargain, and conclude, “Vanity of vanities! Everything is vanity.”] Tell me, you#super[+] who say that all things under the sun are vanity: if you#super[+] really believe what you#super[+] say, why do you#super[+] spend more thoughts and time on the world than you#super[+] do on Christ, heaven, and your#super[+] immortal souls? Why then do you#super[+] neglect your#super[+] duty toward God to get the world? Why then do you#super[+] pursue the world so eagerly, and are so cold in pursuing God, Christ, and holiness? Why then are your#super[+] hearts so exceedingly lifted up when the world comes in and smiles on you#super[+], and so much dejected and cast down when the world frowns on you#super[+] and, like Jonah’s gourd, withers before you#super[+]?

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell much on the uncertainty, the changeableness, and the inconstancy of all things under the sun.#footnote[Riches were never true to anyone who trusted in them; they have deceived men as Job’s brook did the poor traveler in the summer season.] Man himself is only the dream of a dream, the offspring of a fancy, an empty vanity, the elaborate picture of nothing, a poor, feeble, dying flash. All temporal things are as passing as a hasty, headlong torrent, a shadow, a ship, a bird, an arrow, a messenger that runs by. “Why should you set your eyes on that which is not?” says Solomon. And the apostle says, “The present form of this world is passing away.” Heaven#footnote[1 Cor 7:31: σχῆμα implies that there is nothing of any firmness or solid substance in created things.] #emph[alone has a foundation; the earth has none,] but is hung “upon nothing,” as Job speaks. The apostle told Timothy to “instruct those who are rich … not to be conceited and not to put their hope in the uncertainty of wealth.” Riches are like bad servants whose shoes are made of running leather, who will never stay long with one master. As a bird hops from tree to tree, so the honors and riches of this world pass from man to man. Let Job and Nebuchadnezzar testify to this truth, who fell#footnote[The most renowned Frederick lost everything, and begged to be made merely the sexton of the church that he himself had built. I have read of a poor fisherman who, while his nets were drying, slept on the rock and dreamed that he was made a king. He started up all of a sudden and, leaping for joy, fell down from the rock, and in place of his imaginary happiness lost his little portion of pleasures.] from great wealth to great need. No man can promise himself that he will be wealthy until night. One storm at sea, one coal of fire, one false friend, one unadvised word, one false witness may make you a beggar and a prisoner all at once. All the riches and glory of this world are only like smoke and chaff that vanish, “like a dream, like a vision in the night” that does not last; as when a hungry man dreams and thinks that he is eating, and when he awakes his soul is empty; and like a thirsty man who thinks he is drinking, and behold, when he awakes his soul is faint, as the prophet Isaiah says. Where is the glory of Solomon? The splendid buildings of Nebuchadnezzar? The nine hundred chariots of Sisera? The power of Alexander? The authority of Augustus, who commanded the whole world to be taxed? Those who have been most glorious in what men generally count glorious and excellent have had inglorious ends: Samson for strength, Absalom for beauty, Ahithophel for shrewd counsel, Haman for favor, Asahel for swiftness, Alexander for great conquest, and yet poisoned after twelve years. You may see the same in the four mighty kingdoms, the Chaldean, Persian, Greek, and Roman:#footnote[John compares the pomp of this world to the moon, which (#emph[crescit & decrescit]) increases and decreases. Rev 12:1.] how soon they were gone and forgotten! Now rich, now poor; now full, now empty; now in favor, soon out of favor; now honorable, now despised; now health, now sickness; now strength, now weakness. Oh, do not let these uncertain things keep you from those holy services and heavenly employments that may make you happy forever, and make your soul eternally blessed and at ease, when all these passing things bid your soul an everlasting farewell.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that the great things of this world are very hurtful and dangerous to the outward and the inward man, through the#footnote[Henry the Second, hearing that Menz, his chief city, was taken, used this blasphemous speech: “I will never,” he said, “love God any more, who allowed a city so dear to me to be taken from me.” When someone presented Antipater, king of Macedonia, with a book treating of happiness, his answer was (#emph[ou scholazo]) “I have no leisure.”] corruptions that are in the hearts of men. Oh, the rest, the peace, the comfort, the contentment that the things of this world strip many men of! Oh, the fears, the cares, the envy, the malice, the dangers, the harms that they subject men to! They often make men carnally confident: “A rich man’s wealth is his fortified city; it is like a high wall in his imagination.” “In prosperity I said, ‘I will never be shaken.’” They often swell the heart with pride, and make men forget God, and neglect God, and despise the Rock of their salvation: “When Jeshurun grew fat, becoming fat, bloated, and gorged, then he forgot God, and abandoned the God who made him, and scorned the Rock of his salvation,” as Moses spoke. Ah, the time, the thoughts, the spirits that the things of the world consume and spend! Oh, how they hinder the actings of faith on God! How they interrupt our sweet communion with God! How they abate our love to the people of God, and cool our love to the things of God, and bring us to act like those who are most unlike God! Oh, the deadness, the dullness, the barrenness that attends men under great outward blessings! Oh, the riches of the world choke#footnote[That four good mothers beget four bad daughters (great familiarity begets contempt; truth, hatred; virtue, envy; riches, ignorance) is a French proverb. Gen 13:2. כבד. Polycrates bestowed five talents as a gift on one Anacreon, who for two nights afterward was so troubled with care how to keep them and how to spend them that he carried them back again to Polycrates, saying they were not worth the pains he had already taken over them.] the word, so that men live under the most soul-searching and soul-enriching means with lean souls! Though they have full purses, though their chests are full of silver, yet their hearts are empty of grace. In Gen 13:2 it is said that Abraham “was very rich in livestock, in silver and in gold.” In the Hebrew (#emph[Chabbedh]) it reads, Abraham was very heavy, to show that riches are a heavy burden, and many times a hindrance to heaven and happiness. King Henry the Fourth asked the Duke of Alva if he had observed the great eclipse of the sun which had lately happened. “No,” said the duke, “I have so much to do on earth that I have no leisure to look up to heaven.” Ah, if only this were not true of most professing Christians in these days! It is very sad to think how their hearts and time are so much taken up with earthly things that they scarcely have any leisure to look up to heaven, or to look after Christ and the things that belong to their everlasting peace.

Riches, though well gotten, are still only like #emph[manna:] those who gathered less had no lack, and to those who gathered more it was only a trouble and an annoyance. The world is troublesome, and yet it is loved; what would it be if it were peaceable? You#super[+] embrace it, though it is filthy; what would you#super[+] do if it were beautiful? You#super[+] cannot keep your#super[+] hands from the thorns; how eager would you#super[+] then be in gathering the flowers? The world may fitly be likened to the serpent Scytale, of which it is reported that#footnote[Sicily is so full of sweet flowers that dogs cannot hunt there; and what do all the sweet pleasures of this world do but make us lose the scent of heaven?] when she cannot overtake fleeing travelers, she so stuns and dazes them with her beautiful colors that they have no power to pass on until she has stung them. Ah! how many thousands are there now on earth who have found this true by experience, who have spun a fine thread to strangle themselves, both temporally and eternally, by being bewitched by the beauty and splendor of this enticing world!

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that all the happiness of this world is mixed. Our light is mixed with darkness, our joy with sorrow, our pleasures with pain, our honor with dishonor, our riches with need. If our sight is spiritual, clear, and sharp, we may see in the happiness of this world our wine mixed with water, our honey with gall, our sugar with wormwood, and our roses with thorns. Sorrow attends worldly joy,#footnote[“Listen, scholar,” said the harlot to Apuleius, “it is only a bitter-sweet that you are so fond of.” Surely all the things of this world are only bitter-sweets.] danger attends worldly safety, loss attends worldly labors, tears attend worldly purposes. As to these things, men’s hopes are vain, their sorrow certain, and their joy feigned. The apostle calls this world a sea of glass: a sea for its troubles, and glass for its brittleness and bitterness. The honors, profits, pleasures, and delights of this world are true gardens of Adonis, where we can gather nothing but trivial flowers surrounded by many briers.

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to get better acquaintance with, and better assurance of, more blessed and glorious things. What#footnote[Let heaven be a man’s object, and earth will soon be his abject.] raised up their spirits, in the tenth and eleventh chapters of Hebrews, to trample on all the beauty, splendor, and glory of the world was their acquaintance with #emph[and assurance of better and more lasting things.] “They joyfully accepted the confiscation of their property, knowing that they had in heaven a better and more lasting possession. They#footnote[Luther, being once in some need, happened to be sent a good sum of money unexpectedly by a nobleman of Germany. Being somewhat dismayed at this, he said, “I fear that God will give me my reward here; but I protest, I will not be so satisfied.”] were looking forward to the city with foundations, whose architect and builder is God. And they were longing for a better country, a heavenly one. They saw Him who is invisible, and were looking ahead to their reward.” And this made them count all the glory and splendor of this world too poor and contemptible for them to set their hearts on. The main reason why men dote on the world, and damn their souls to get the world, is that they are not acquainted with a greater glory. Men ate acorns until they became acquainted with the use of wheat. Ah, if men were better acquainted with what union and communion with God means, what it is to “have a new name, and a new stone, that no one knows except the one who has it”; if they only tasted more of heaven, and lived more in heaven, and had more glorious hopes of going to heaven, ah! #emph[how easily would they have the moon under their feet!] It was an excellent saying of Louis of Bavaria, emperor of Germany,#footnote[Hujusmodi comparandae sunt opes quae cum naufragio simul enatent.] that such goods are worth getting and owning as will not sink or wash away if a shipwreck happens, but will wade and swim out with us. It is recorded of Lazarus that after his resurrection from the dead he was never seen to laugh; his thoughts and affections were so fixed in heaven, though his body was on earth. And therefore he could not help but slight temporal things,#footnote[There are, says Augustine, (#emph[bona Throni]) goods of the throne; and there are (#emph[bona Scabelli]) goods of the footstool.] his heart being so bent and set on eternal things. There are goods of the throne of grace, such as God, Christ, the Spirit, adoption, justification, remission of sin, peace with God, and peace with conscience; and there are goods of the footstool, such as honors, riches, the favor of men, and the other comforts and conveniences of this life. Now he who has acquaintance with and assurance of the goods of the throne will easily trample on the goods of the footstool. Ah, that you#super[+] would make it your#super[+] business, your#super[+] work, to attend more to, and to make more sure to your#super[+] own souls, the great#footnote[When Basil was tempted with money and preferment, he said, “Give me money that may last forever, and glory that may eternally flourish; for the form of this world passes away, like the waters of a river that runs by a city.”] things of eternity, which will yield you#super[+] joy in life, and peace in death, and a crown of righteousness in the day of Christ’s appearing; and which will lift up your#super[+] souls above all the beauty and splendor of this bewitching world, and raise your#super[+] feet above other men’s heads! When a man comes to be assured of a crown, a scepter, the royal robes, etc., he then begins to have low, paltry, and contemptible thoughts of those things that he highly prized before. So assurance of greater and more glorious things will breed in the soul a holy scorn and contempt of all these poor, paltry things, which the soul once valued above God, Christ, and heaven, etc.

The seventh #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that true happiness and satisfaction are not to be had in the enjoyment of any worldly good. True happiness is too big and too glorious a thing to be#footnote[True happiness lies only in our enjoyment of a suitable good, a pure good, a total good, and an eternal good; and God alone is such a good, and only such a good can satisfy the soul of man.] found in anything below that God who is a Christian’s (#emph[summum bonum]) chief good. The blessed angels, those glittering courtiers, have all happiness and blessedness, and yet they have neither gold nor silver nor jewels, nor any of the beauty and splendor of this world. Certainly, if happiness were to be found in these things, the Lord Jesus, who is the rightful and royal heir of all things, would have exchanged his cradle for a crown, his birth-chamber (a stable) for a royal palace, his poverty for plenty, his despised followers for shining courtiers, and his lowly provisions for the choicest delicacies, etc. Certainly, happiness does not lie in those things that a man#footnote[Philosophers could say that he was never a happy man who might afterward become miserable.] may enjoy and yet be miserable forever. Now a man may be great and godless, like Pharaoh; honorable and damnable, like Saul; rich and miserable, like the rich man, etc. Therefore happiness does not lie in these things. Certainly happiness does not lie in those things that cannot comfort a man on his deathbed. Is it honors, riches, or friends, etc., that can comfort you when you come to die? Or is it not rather faith in the blood of Christ, the witness of the#footnote[Gregory the Great used to say that he is poor whose soul is empty of grace, not whose coffers are empty of money.] Spirit of Christ, the sense and feeling of the love and favor of Christ, and the hope of reigning eternally with Christ? Can happiness lie in those things that cannot give us health, or strength, or ease, or a good night’s rest, or an hour’s sleep, or a good appetite? Why, all the honors, riches, and delights of this world cannot give these poor things to us; therefore certainly happiness does not lie in the enjoyment of them, etc. And surely happiness is not to be found in those things that cannot satisfy the souls of men; and none of these things can satisfy the soul of man. “He who loves money is never satisfied by money, and he who loves wealth#footnote[#emph[Anima rationalis caeteris omnibus occupari potest impleri non potest.] The rational soul may be busied about other things, but it cannot be filled with them.] is never satisfied by income. This too is vanity,” says the wise man. The barren womb, the leech’s daughter, the grave, and hell will be satisfied as soon as the soul of man will be by the enjoyment of any worldly good. Some one thing or another will always be lacking to the soul that has nothing but outward good to live on. You may as soon fill a bag with wisdom, a chest with virtue, or a circle with a triangle, as the heart of man with anything here below. A man may have enough of the world to sink him, but he can never have enough to satisfy him, etc.

The eighth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider the dignity of the soul. Oh, the soul of man is worth more than a thousand worlds! It is the greatest abasing of it#footnote[Plutarch tells of Themistocles that he did not count it fitting for his rank to stoop down to take up the spoils the enemies had scattered in flight, but said to one of his followers (Ἀνελοῦ σαυτῷ σὺ γὰρ οὐκ εἶ Θεμιστοκλῆς), “You may, for you are not Themistocles.” Oh, what a sad thing it is that a heathen should set his feet on the very things on which most professing Christians set their hearts, and for the gain of which many, like Balaam, run the risk of losing their immortal souls forever!] that can be, to let it dote on a little shining earth, on a little painted beauty and fading glory, when it is capable of union with Christ, of communion with God, and of enjoying the eternal vision of God. Seneca could say, “I am too great, and born to greater things, than to be a slave to my body.” Oh, say for your#super[+] part, “My soul is too great, and born to greater things, than that I should confine it to a heap of white and yellow clay.” I have dwelt the longer on the #emph[remedies] that may help us against this dangerous device of Satan, because he usually does more hurt to the souls of men by this device than he does by all other devices. To close, I wish, as Chrysostom once did, that this sentence, Eccl 2:11 (“Yet when I considered all the works that my hands had accomplished and what I had toiled to achieve, I found everything to be vanity, a pursuit of the wind; there was nothing to be gained under the sun”), were engraved on the doorposts through which you#super[+] enter; on the tables where you#super[+] sit; on the dishes out of which you#super[+] eat; on the cups out of which you#super[+] drink; on the bedsteads where you#super[+] lie; on the walls of the houses where you#super[+] dwell; on the garments which you#super[+] wear; on the heads of the horses on which you#super[+] ride; and on the foreheads of all whom you#super[+] meet; so that your#super[+] souls may not be kept away by the beauty and splendor of the world from those holy and heavenly services that may make you#super[+] blessed while you#super[+] live and happy when you#super[+] die; and so that you#super[+] may breathe out your#super[+] last into his bosom who lives forever, and who will make those happy forever who prefer Christ’s spiritual and eternal things above all temporal, passing things.
